# Every path whose bytes `npm run render:check` hashes, pinned against end-of-line conversion.
#
# The font manifest records a SHA-256 for `public/fonts/fonts.css` and for each licence text, and
# the gate re-hashes what is on disk against it. Git for Windows ships `core.autocrlf=true` in its
# *system* config (`C:/Program Files/Git/etc/gitconfig`, measured with `git config --list
# --show-origin`), so a fresh checkout writes those text files with CRLF while the repository
# stores them with LF. On a clean worktree of 0a86e89 `fonts.css` came out 29,364 bytes with 724 CR
# bytes against this tree's 28,640 and none, and the gate reported 13 findings (six licence digest
# pairs plus the stylesheet) — while the same manifest and the same committed blobs were green.
#
# That makes the signed provenance record true only on a machine that happened to already hold the
# files, which is the opposite of what a digest is for. The three rules below describe the digested
# set exactly: what the gate hashes is what cannot be re-written at checkout.
#
# Scoped to `public/fonts/` on purpose. A repository-wide line-ending policy would re-normalise text
# committed with CRLF elsewhere, which is a different and far larger decision than this repair.
public/fonts/fonts.css text eol=lf
public/fonts/licences/*.txt text eol=lf
public/fonts/files/*.woff2 binary

# The same defect, in the other digest this stage keeps. `render_config_hash` (§7.2's staleness query)
# is built partly from the bytes of three source files, read straight off disk by
# `scripts/stage9-render-worker.mjs:446-449`. Nothing pins those paths, so on this host a checkout
# re-writes them with CRLF and the hash changes while the repository does not. Measured with
# `node q9-logs/eol-checkout-proof.mjs` (output `q9-logs/eol-checkout-proof.txt`), which writes the
# three paths out of the index with `git checkout-index` — the conversion a clone applies:
#   src/render/outputGeometry.ts  worktree 15,431 bytes / 0 CR / 8106e938…  checkout 15,701 / 270 / 7237beb0…
#   src/render/render.css          worktree  8,514 bytes / 0 CR / e0b13812…  checkout  8,652 / 138 / 940a7c53…
#   src/index.css                  worktree  4,493 bytes / 148 CR / 09f9b317…  checkout identical
# The first two disagree with themselves, so an artefact rendered on a Windows clone would carry a
# different `render_config_hash` from the same committed tree rendered on a host that leaves the files
# alone — and §13's "is this file stale?" answer would depend on the operator's git config. The third
# is already CRLF in the worktree but LF in the index (`i/lf w/crlf`), which is the same divergence
# seen from the other side: it is why pinning it to `eol=lf` converges every host on the LF digest
# rather than freezing whatever this machine happens to hold.
#
# `docs/architecture/render-outputs.md` is read by the same worker for the browser pin but is not
# digested into the hash, and its parser tolerates CRLF — `readBrowserPin`'s `\s*` before `$` absorbs
# a `\r` (`q9-logs/pin-crlf-check.mjs`: lf, crlf and crlf-with-trailing-spaces all answer
# `{"ok":true,"major":155}`). It is left alone deliberately, as are the rest of the repository's text
# files, for the reason stated above: this is a rule for the digested set, not a line-ending policy.
src/render/outputGeometry.ts text eol=lf
src/render/render.css text eol=lf
src/index.css text eol=lf
